  "notes": "The lane's rhythmic and harmonic extremist, and every number here sits at the section's edge. The band is the catalogue's own 72–135 and the keys are the named G major/G minor and F minor. Drums: \"live kit with **odd and shifting metres and polyrhythm** — model 5/4, 7/8, 6/8-over-4/4 and bar-length changes inside a section, with the backbeat still nominally on 2&4 when the metre is 4. Perrin Moss plays dense ghost-note 16ths, displaced snares, and cross-rhythms against the bass\". The metre changes are not encodable, so what is authored is everything else: the highest kick syncopation and tresillo bias in the section, the highest ghost probability (0.74 across all eight 16ths), a displaced snare, and a found-sound percussion layer of 4–8 hits a bar across five lanes. Harmony is \"the section's most advanced: chromatic mediant motion, quartal and cluster voicings, modal shifts mid-phrase, and reharmonisation of a repeated melody — chord changes can occur every bar or every two beats\", so no families are authored and `chordDurationBeats` runs 1–4, the shortest anywhere here. Melody is \"high soprano with very wide leaps, extremely high note-density, non-repeating phrase lengths\" — density 7–14, a leap share of 0.22 (five times the lane's), a three-octave register and `endVariation`. Bass is \"Paul Bender's played electric bass, mixed loud and treated as a lead voice — heavily syncopated, chord-tone-driven, moving with the reharmonisation, with distortion and octave effects\", hence a root probability of 0.22 and the chord voicing lifted to 50 to clear it. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all drum probabilities and velocities and every melody figure; the research marks the specific metre list as idiom inference.",
